# AI-written paragraphs (generator: Claude, written in-session by the assistant, 2026-11-21).
# Prompts used: simple = "Explain <topic> in simple English for a beginner, one paragraph."
#               formal = "Write one formal, encyclopedic paragraph about <topic>."
# topic -> (simple_english, formal)
AI_A = {
"Photosynthesis": (
"Photosynthesis is how plants make their own food. A plant takes in sunlight through its leaves, water through its roots, and carbon dioxide from the air. Inside tiny parts of the leaf called chloroplasts, the plant uses the energy from the sunlight to turn the water and carbon dioxide into sugar. The sugar gives the plant energy to grow. As a bonus, the plant releases oxygen into the air, which people and animals need to breathe. That is why plants are so important for life on Earth.",
"Photosynthesis is the biochemical process by which plants, algae, and certain bacteria convert light energy into chemical energy stored in organic compounds. Occurring primarily within the chloroplasts of plant cells, the process comprises light-dependent reactions, which generate ATP and NADPH while splitting water molecules, and the Calvin cycle, which fixes atmospheric carbon dioxide into carbohydrates. Molecular oxygen is released as a by-product. Photosynthesis underpins nearly all food chains and is largely responsible for the oxygen content of Earth's atmosphere."),
"Gravity": (
"Gravity is the force that pulls things toward each other. The Earth is very big, so it pulls everything toward its center. That is why when you drop a ball, it falls down instead of floating away. Gravity also keeps the Moon moving around the Earth and keeps the planets moving around the Sun. The more mass an object has, the stronger its pull. This is why you feel much lighter on the Moon, which is smaller than Earth and has weaker gravity.",
"Gravity is a fundamental interaction by which objects with mass or energy attract one another. In classical mechanics, as formulated by Isaac Newton, the gravitational force between two bodies is proportional to the product of their masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance separating them. Albert Einstein's general theory of relativity later redefined gravity as the curvature of spacetime caused by mass and energy. Gravity governs the structure of planets, stars, and galaxies and determines the orbits of celestial bodies."),
"Volcano": (
"A volcano is an opening in the Earth's surface where hot melted rock, called magma, can escape. Deep underground, it is so hot that rock melts. When pressure builds up, the magma rises and bursts out as lava, ash, and gas. Over time, the lava cools and hardens, building up a mountain shape around the opening. Some volcanoes erupt often, while others stay quiet for hundreds of years. Volcanoes can be dangerous, but their ash also makes soil rich and good for growing crops.",
"A volcano is a rupture in the crust of a planetary-mass object through which molten rock, volcanic ash, and gases escape from a subsurface magma chamber. Volcanoes are most commonly located along tectonic plate boundaries, such as subduction zones and mid-ocean ridges, as well as above mantle hotspots. The style of an eruption, whether effusive or explosive, depends largely on the viscosity and gas content of the magma. Volcanic activity shapes landscapes, influences climate, and poses significant hazards to human populations."),
"Democracy": (
"Democracy is a way of running a country where the people have the power. Instead of one king or leader making all the rules, citizens get to vote for the people who will lead them. In many countries, people vote for a president or for members of a parliament. Everyone's vote counts the same. In a democracy, people are also free to speak their minds and to disagree with the government. This helps make sure that leaders listen to the people they serve.",
"Democracy is a system of government in which political power is vested in the people, who exercise it either directly or through elected representatives. Its core principles include free and fair elections, political equality, the rule of law, and the protection of civil liberties. Modern democracies typically take the form of representative systems, which may be organized as parliamentary or presidential republics or as constitutional monarchies. The concept originated in ancient Athens and has since evolved considerably, with varying institutional arrangements across different societies."),
"Inflation": (
"Inflation means that prices for things go up over time. If a loaf of bread cost one dollar last year and costs a dollar and ten cents this year, that is inflation. When prices rise, the same amount of money buys less than before. A little inflation each year is normal and can even be healthy for an economy. But if prices rise too fast, it can be hard for families to pay for food, rent, and other things they need. Central banks try to keep inflation steady.",
"Inflation is the sustained increase in the general price level of goods and services in an economy over a period of time, resulting in a decline in the purchasing power of a currency. It is typically measured using indices such as the consumer price index. Economists attribute inflation to various causes, including excess demand relative to supply, rising production costs, and expansionary monetary policy. Central banks commonly seek to maintain low and stable inflation, often targeting a rate of around two percent, through adjustments to interest rates and other monetary instruments."),
"Vaccine": (
"A vaccine is a medicine that helps your body learn how to fight a germ before you ever get sick from it. A vaccine contains a weak or dead piece of the germ, or a small part of it. It cannot make you ill, but your body sees it and builds defenses. Later, if the real germ enters your body, your immune system remembers it and can fight it off quickly. Vaccines have helped stop serious diseases like polio and measles from spreading to many people.",
"A vaccine is a biological preparation that provides active acquired immunity against a particular infectious disease. It typically contains an agent that resembles the disease-causing microorganism, such as weakened or inactivated forms of the pathogen, its surface proteins, or genetic instructions for producing antigens. Upon administration, the vaccine stimulates the immune system to recognize the agent as foreign, to produce antibodies, and to develop immunological memory. Widespread vaccination has led to the eradication of smallpox and substantial reductions in the incidence of many other diseases."),
"DNA": (
"DNA is a very long molecule found inside almost every cell of your body. It holds the instructions that tell your body how to grow and work. You can think of it like a recipe book. The recipes are written in a code made of four small building blocks, called bases. These instructions decide things like your eye color and how tall you might be. You got half of your DNA from your mother and half from your father, which is why you share features with your family.",
"Deoxyribonucleic acid, commonly known as DNA, is a macromolecule that carries the genetic information used in the growth, development, functioning, and reproduction of all known organisms. Its structure is a double helix composed of two antiparallel polynucleotide strands, each consisting of nucleotides that contain one of four nitrogenous bases: adenine, thymine, guanine, or cytosine. The sequence of these bases encodes genetic instructions. DNA is replicated prior to cell division and transcribed into RNA to direct the synthesis of proteins."),
"Algorithm": (
"An algorithm is a set of clear steps for solving a problem or finishing a task. A recipe for baking a cake is a simple example, because it tells you exactly what to do and in what order. Computers use algorithms too. For example, when you search for something online, an algorithm decides which results to show first. A good algorithm gives the right answer every time and does not take too long. Programmers write algorithms so that computers know exactly how to do a job.",
"An algorithm is a finite sequence of well-defined, unambiguous instructions designed to perform a specific computation or to solve a class of problems. Algorithms accept zero or more inputs and produce an output, terminating after a finite number of steps. They are commonly evaluated in terms of correctness and computational complexity, which describes the time and memory resources required as a function of input size. The study of algorithms is a central subject in computer science and underlies applications ranging from sorting and searching to cryptography and machine learning."),
"Internet": (
"The Internet is a huge network that connects computers all around the world. When you visit a website, send a message, or watch a video, your device sends a request through this network, and another computer sends back the answer. Information travels in small pieces called packets, which can take different paths and are put back together at the end. The Internet is not the same as the Web. The Web is just one of the many things that run on the Internet, along with email and online games.",
"The Internet is a global system of interconnected computer networks that communicate using the standardized Internet protocol suite, commonly referred to as TCP/IP. It carries a vast range of information resources and services, including the World Wide Web, electronic mail, and file sharing. Data is transmitted in packets that are routed across a decentralized infrastructure of cables, routers, and servers operated by numerous organizations. Originating from research projects such as ARPANET in the late 1960s, the Internet has become integral to modern communication, commerce, and culture."),
"Cryptography": (
"Cryptography is the art of keeping messages secret. It works by scrambling a message so that only the right person can read it. The scrambled message is called ciphertext, and the process of scrambling is called encryption. To turn it back into a normal message, you need a secret key. When you shop online or send a private message, cryptography protects your information from people who should not see it. Without it, passwords and credit card numbers would be easy for others to steal.",
"Cryptography is the practice and study of techniques for secure communication in the presence of adversarial behavior. Modern cryptography relies on mathematical constructs to provide confidentiality, integrity, authentication, and non-repudiation of information. It encompasses symmetric-key algorithms, in which a single secret key is used for both encryption and decryption, and public-key algorithms, which employ mathematically related key pairs. Cryptographic protocols are fundamental to numerous applications, including secure web browsing, digital signatures, and electronic payment systems."),
"Machine learning": (
"Machine learning is a way of teaching computers to learn from examples instead of giving them a list of exact rules. For instance, to teach a computer to recognize cats, you show it thousands of pictures labeled cat or not cat. Over time, the computer finds patterns that help it tell the difference. Then it can guess correctly on new pictures it has never seen. Machine learning is used in many everyday things, like movie suggestions, voice assistants, and spam filters in your email.",
"Machine learning is a subfield of artificial intelligence concerned with the development of algorithms that enable computer systems to improve their performance on a task through experience, without being explicitly programmed. Approaches are broadly categorized as supervised learning, in which models are trained on labeled data, unsupervised learning, which identifies structure in unlabeled data, and reinforcement learning, in which agents learn through interaction with an environment. Machine learning methods are widely applied in areas such as image recognition, natural language processing, and predictive analytics."),
"Database": (
"A database is a place where information is stored in an organized way so that it is easy to find and use. Think of it like a very big digital filing cabinet. A school might use a database to keep track of students, their classes, and their grades. A special program called a database management system helps people add, change, and look up the information. Because the data is organized, a computer can search through millions of records in just a moment.",
"A database is an organized collection of structured information, or data, typically stored and accessed electronically from a computer system. Data is managed by a database management system, which provides mechanisms for defining, querying, updating, and administering the stored information while ensuring integrity, security, and concurrent access. Relational databases organize data into tables with rows and columns and are queried using Structured Query Language, whereas non-relational systems employ alternative models such as documents, key-value pairs, or graphs."),
"Operating system": (
"An operating system is the main program that runs on a computer and lets you use it. It acts like a manager. It helps all the different parts of the computer, like the screen, keyboard, and memory, work together. It also lets other programs, such as a web browser or a game, run properly. Windows, macOS, Android, and Linux are all examples of operating systems. Without one, you would have no easy way to open files, run apps, or connect to the Internet.",
"An operating system is system software that manages computer hardware and software resources and provides common services for application programs. Its principal functions include process scheduling, memory management, file system administration, and the control of input and output devices. The operating system also provides an interface, either graphical or command-line, through which users interact with the machine. Prominent examples include Microsoft Windows, macOS, Linux distributions, and the Android and iOS systems used on mobile devices."),
"Computer virus": (
"A computer virus is a harmful program that can copy itself and spread from one computer to another. It often hides inside a file or a program that looks safe. When you open that file, the virus can start working. Some viruses just cause annoying problems, but others can delete your files or steal your private information. You can protect yourself by keeping your software up to date, using antivirus programs, and not opening strange links or email attachments.",
"A computer virus is a type of malicious software that, when executed, replicates by inserting copies of itself into other programs, files, or the boot sector of a storage device. Infection typically requires user action, such as running an infected executable or opening a compromised document. Depending on its payload, a virus may corrupt or delete data, consume system resources, or provide unauthorized access to attackers. Defensive measures include antivirus software, regular system updates, and adherence to safe computing practices."),
"Climate change": (
"Climate change means that the usual weather patterns of Earth are slowly changing over many years. Right now, the planet is getting warmer. This is mostly because people burn coal, oil, and gas for energy, which puts extra gases like carbon dioxide into the air. These gases trap heat, just like a blanket. A warmer planet can cause stronger storms, longer droughts, and rising seas as ice melts. Using clean energy and planting trees are some of the ways we can help slow it down.",
"Climate change refers to long-term shifts in global or regional temperatures and weather patterns. While natural factors such as volcanic activity and variations in solar output have influenced the climate throughout Earth's history, scientific consensus attributes the rapid warming observed since the mid-twentieth century primarily to anthropogenic emissions of greenhouse gases, notably carbon dioxide and methane. Consequences include rising sea levels, the retreat of glaciers, increased frequency of extreme weather events, and shifts in ecosystems. International efforts such as the Paris Agreement seek to limit global temperature rise."),
"Evolution": (
"Evolution is the way living things change slowly over very long periods of time. Within a group of animals or plants, no two are exactly alike. Some have traits that help them survive better, such as faster legs or better camouflage. Those animals live longer and have more babies, and they pass those helpful traits on. Over many generations, the whole group changes. This process is called natural selection. It explains why there are so many different kinds of living things on Earth today.",
"Evolution is the change in the heritable characteristics of biological populations over successive generations. The process is driven by mechanisms including natural selection, genetic drift, mutation, and gene flow. Natural selection, as articulated by Charles Darwin and Alfred Russel Wallace, occurs when individuals with advantageous heritable traits achieve greater reproductive success, causing those traits to become more common in a population. Evolution accounts for the diversity of life and the common ancestry of organisms, and it is supported by evidence from the fossil record, comparative anatomy, and molecular biology."),
"Black hole": (
"A black hole is a place in space where gravity is so strong that nothing can escape from it, not even light. Most black holes form when a very big star runs out of fuel and collapses in on itself. All of its mass gets squeezed into a tiny space. Because no light comes out, we cannot see black holes directly. Scientists find them by watching how nearby stars and gas move around them. There is even a giant black hole in the middle of our own galaxy.",
"A black hole is a region of spacetime in which gravity is so intense that no matter or electromagnetic radiation can escape once it crosses the boundary known as the event horizon. Stellar-mass black holes are believed to form through the gravitational collapse of massive stars at the end of their life cycles, while supermassive black holes, with masses of millions to billions of solar masses, reside at the centers of most large galaxies. Black holes are predicted by general relativity and have been observed indirectly through their gravitational effects and, more recently, through gravitational waves and imaging."),
"Electricity": (
"Electricity is a kind of energy that comes from tiny particles called electrons moving from place to place. When electrons flow through a wire, we call that an electric current. We use this current to light our homes, charge our phones, and run machines. Electricity is made in power plants using things like wind, water, sunshine, coal, or gas. It then travels through long wires to our homes. It is very useful, but it can also be dangerous, so always be careful around plugs and wires.",
"Electricity is the set of physical phenomena associated with the presence and motion of electric charge. It is manifested through effects such as electric current, electric fields, and electromagnetism. In electrical conductors, current typically arises from the movement of electrons driven by a potential difference, or voltage. Electricity is generated at power stations by converting other forms of energy, including mechanical, chemical, and solar energy, and is distributed through transmission networks. Its applications are foundational to modern industry, lighting, communications, and computing."),
}
